    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Post-traumatic epilepsy is a recurrent seizure disorder marked by over-excitation in the brain after injury. Allopregnanolone (ALLO), a metabolite of progesterone, has previously been used as an anticonvulsant, though not as the first line of treatment. It has also been shown to have neuroprotective effects in other neurodegenerative diseases such as Parkinson’s and amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS). It has not been extensively studied as a possible neuroprotectant in epilepsy. Currently, little is known about allopregnanolone’s mechanism of action. Using the undercut (UC) model of injury in mice, this study demonstrates allopregnanolone’s anatomical effects on post-traumatic brain tissue and further explores its mechanism of action. After partial neocortical isolation (UC injury) several allopregnanolone injections were given and immunohistochemistry was used to visualize and compare density of molecules relevant to inhibition in the brain amongst treatment groups, namely BDNF, Na+/K+ ATPase (NKA), and the GABA receptor (GABAR) delta subunit. It was originally proposed to assess the expression of BDNF, a neurotrophic factor known to be decreased after brain trauma; but, this was not possible due to time constraints. However, a literature review, past studies in this lab and preliminary images suggest that BDNF does decrease after undercut injury. As expected, the density of NKA in UC tissue significantly decreases compared to naïve tissue. Interestingly, in these experiments ALLO did not increase the expression of NKA and even slightly decreased it further in injured tissue. It was originally thought that the increased presence of NKA should make the membrane potentials of inhibitory interneurons less negative and promote inhibition in the brain, protecting against epileptogenesis. In the presence of ANA 12, a TrkB receptor blocker, there were small decreases in NKA that did not reach statistical significance. This indicates that ALLO’s mechanism of action may not primarily involve NKA. As another potential target of allopregnanolone, the GABA receptor (GABAR) delta subunit was studied as a modulator of inhibitory activity. There was an insignificant decrease in the subunit after injury and a slight recovery after treatment with ALLO. These results highlight the complicated nature of the possible targets and mechanism of action for allopregnanolone as a potential protectant against post-traumatic epilepsy.",
